problem_statement:
Progress.Stop() method causes goroutine leak

#### Description

The `Progress.Stop()` method doesn't properly synchronize with the background goroutine, potentially causing goroutine leaks when stopping progress tracking.

#### Steps/Code to Reproduce

```go
package main

import (
    "time"
    "github.com/gosuri/uiprogress"
)

func main() {
    progress := uiprogress.New()
    bar := progress.AddBar(100)

    progress.Start()

    // Do some work
    for i := 0; i < 100; i++ {
        bar.Incr()
        time.Sleep(10 * time.Millisecond)
    }

    progress.Stop()

    // The goroutine may not have properly terminated
    // This can lead to resource leaks in long-running applications
}
```

When calling `Stop()`, the method sends a signal to terminate the background goroutine but doesn't wait for confirmation that it has actually stopped. This breaks the synchronization pattern and can result in the goroutine continuing to run after `Stop()` returns.

